---
/**
 * Markets feature — the first database census (blueprint R1.3:
 * markets long-form; H1/H3 lead item). EVERY figure on this page is
 * computed from the product/protocol/vendor records at build time —
 * the prose is written around slots the records fill, so the feature
 * cannot drift from the database it describes. The limits of the
 * sample are stated on the page: a curated, datasheet-bound census
 * is evidence about what makers document and sell, not a shipment
 * or market-share measurement.
 */
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import PortalLayout from '../../layouts/PortalLayout.astro';
import { buildSeo } from '../../utils/seo';
import {
	productGate,
	PRODUCT_TYPE_LABELS,
	protocolLabel,
	type ProductRecord,
} from '../../utils/products';

const records = (await getCollection('products')).map((item) => item.data);
const vendors = (await getCollection('vendors')).map((item) => item.data);
const total = records.length;
const indexable = records.filter((record) => productGate(record).indexable);
const rowsOnly = total - indexable.length;

const countBy = <K extends string>(fn: (record: ProductRecord) => K | K[]) => {
	const map = new Map<string, number>();
	for (const record of records) {
		for (const key of [fn(record)].flat()) map.set(key, (map.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);
	}
	return [...map.entries()].sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1] || a[0].localeCompare(b[0]));
};

const byType = countBy((record) => PRODUCT_TYPE_LABELS[record.productType]);
const gateways = byType.find(([label]) => label === 'Gateway')?.[1] ?? 0;
const byProtocol = countBy((record) => record.protocols.map(protocolLabel));
const byStatus = countBy((record) => record.status);
const activeCount = byStatus.find(([label]) => label === 'active')?.[1] ?? 0;
const endOfLine = total - activeCount;

const OPEN_PROTOCOLS = new Set([
	'wirelesshart',
	'isa100',
	'lorawan',
	'io-link-wireless',
	'lte',
	'lte-m',
	'nb-iot',
	'5g',
	'wifi',
]);
const openRecords = records.filter((record) =>
	record.protocols.some((slug) => OPEN_PROTOCOLS.has(slug)),
).length;
const proprietaryRecords = records.filter((record) =>
	record.protocols.some((slug) => slug.startsWith('proprietary')),
).length;

const CELLULAR = new Set(['lte', 'lte-m', 'nb-iot', 'gprs', '3g']);
const cellularRecords = records.filter((record) =>
	record.protocols.some((slug) => CELLULAR.has(slug)),
).length;
const legacyCellular = records.filter((record) =>
	record.protocols.some((slug) => slug === 'gprs' || slug === '3g'),
).length;

const withSensitivity = records.filter((record) => record.rxSensitivityDbm != null).length;
const withRange = records.filter((record) => record.rangeClass != null).length;
const withIp = records.filter((record) => record.ipRating != null).length;

const perVendor = new Map<string, number>();
for (const record of records) perVendor.set(record.vendor, (perVendor.get(record.vendor) ?? 0) + 1);
const deepestBenches = [...perVendor.values()].filter((count) => count >= 3).length;

const pct = (n: number) => `${Math.round((n / total) * 100)}%`;

const seo = buildSeo(
	{
		id: 'markets/database-census-2026',
		locale: 'en',
		title: 'Forty records in: what the industrial wireless database actually shows',
		description:
			'The first census of this site\u2019s product database: gateways outnumber modules, proprietary 900 MHz ties LoRaWAN, and the legacy tail is real. Every figure computed from the records at build time.',
		kind: 'content',
		authorKey: 'editorial-desk',
		editorKey: 'review-desk',
		datePublished: '2026-11-19',
		breadcrumbs: [
			{ name: 'Home', path: '/' },
			{ name: 'Markets', path: '/markets/' },
			{ name: 'Forty records in: what the industrial wireless database actually shows', path: '/markets/database-census-2026/' },
		],
	},
	'/markets/database-census-2026/',
);
---

<PortalLayout seo={seo}>
	<article class="feature">
		<p class="kicker">Markets · Database census · 2026-11-19</p>
		<h1>Forty records in: what the industrial wireless database actually shows</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			This site's product database just reached {total} records — every one transcribed
			from a manufacturer's datasheet or official product documentation, every figure
			traceable to its source. A database built that way is a peculiar kind of market
			evidence: it cannot tell you what ships, but it can tell you, precisely, what the
			industry bothers to document. Here is what the first census shows.
		</p>

		<section class="panel quick-answer">
			<div class="panel-head">The census in one paragraph</div>
			<div class="panel-body">
				<p>
					Of the {total} records, {gateways} ({pct(gateways)}) are gateways — the
					single largest class, ahead of {byType.find(([l]) => l === 'Wireless I/O module')?.[1]} wireless
					I/O modules. {openRecords} records run an open standard (WirelessHART,
					ISA100.11a, LoRaWAN, cellular or Wi-Fi); {proprietaryRecords} carry a
					proprietary radio, and proprietary 900 MHz alone ties LoRaWAN at the top of
					the protocol table. {cellularRecords} records are cellular, and {endOfLine} of
					the {total} are already legacy or discontinued models that plants still run.
					{indexable.length} records carry a complete enough source set to earn a full
					specification page; {rowsOnly} sit in the database as rows until their
					manufacturers' documents support one.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<h2>Gateways are the center of gravity</h2>
		<p>
			The type table is the census's clearest signal. Gateways — protocol translators,
			network managers, LoRaWAN and cellular aggregation points — make up {pct(gateways)} of
			everything recorded. That is not how vendor catalogues present themselves; they
			lead with field devices. But a gateway is the one product in a wireless system
			that cannot be substituted late, the point where the radio network meets the
			plant network, and the records reflect where engineering attention actually
			concentrates.
		</p>
		<div class="table-wrap">
			<table class="data">
				<caption>Records by product type (computed from the database)</caption>
				<thead>
					<tr><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col" class="num">Records</th><th scope="col" class="num">Share</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					{
						byType.map(([label, count]) => (
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">{label}</th>
								<td class="num">{count}</td>
								<td class="num">{pct(count)}</td>
							</tr>
						))
					}
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>

		<h2>The protocol table has two industries in it</h2>
		<p>
			Read down the protocol counts and two different industries appear. The first is
			the standards industry: WirelessHART, ISA100.11a and LoRaWAN records are built by
			many makers against published specifications, with certification programs behind
			them. The second is the proprietary industry: single-vendor sub-GHz and 2.4 GHz
			radios, mostly in oil, gas and utility telemetry, where the radio <em>is</em> the
			product and interoperability is a gateway's problem. Proprietary 900 MHz tying
			LoRaWAN at the top of the table is the census's most quotable number — and the
			one most likely to surprise readers who only follow the standards press.
		</p>
		<div class="table-wrap">
			<table class="data">
				<caption>Records by protocol (a record with two radios counts under both)</caption>
				<thead>
					<tr><th scope="col">Protocol</th><th scope="col" class="num">Records</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					{
						byProtocol.map(([label, count]) => (
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">{label}</th>
								<td class="num">{count}</td>
							</tr>
						))
					}
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p>
			Cellular deserves its own line: {cellularRecords} records speak a cellular dialect,
			and {legacyCellular} of them still list GPRS or 3G — networks already switched off in
			several markets. Those records are not errors. They are the installed base,
			documented: routers and modems that plants bought for a decade of service and
			have not finished depreciating.
		</p>

		<h2>The legacy tail is a feature of this industry, not a data problem</h2>
		<p>
			{endOfLine} of the {total} records ({pct(endOfLine)}) are legacy or discontinued
			models. A consumer catalogue would delist them. An industrial database keeps
			them, because the reader searching for a discontinued gateway's sensitivity
			figure is usually standing in front of one. The census counts them openly rather
			than quietly curating them away — the same reason every record keeps its source
			and check date attached.
		</p>

		<h2>What datasheets refuse to say</h2>
		<p>
			The census also counts the silences. Receiver sensitivity — the single figure a
			link budget needs most — is stated in {withSensitivity} of {total} records.
			A range figure with its conditions attached (the only kind this database
			accepts) appears in {withRange}. An ingress rating appears in {withIp}. The
			missing figures are not missing research; they are missing manufacturer
			statements, and the database leaves the fields empty rather than estimating.
			Across {vendors.length} listed makers, {deepestBenches} vendors have three or
			more products recorded — depth is concentrated in the established field-network
			names, not spread evenly.
		</p>

		<section class="panel limits">
			<div class="panel-head">What this census cannot prove</div>
			<div class="panel-body">
				<p>
					This is a curated database, not a shipment sample. Records enter it when a
					manufacturer publishes documentation complete enough to transcribe, and
					coverage follows this site's editorial scope — process wireless, LPWAN and
					telemetry first. Counts here describe what the industry documents and
					offers, weighted by documentation quality; they are not market shares,
					installed-base counts or sales rankings, and no figure on this page should
					be cited as one. Where the industry does publish measured evidence — the
					LoRa Alliance's tender tracking, for instance — our{' '}
					<a href="/news/">news line</a> reports it from the source.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<h2>Method, and how to check it</h2>
		<p>
			Every count on this page is generated from the records at build time — change a
			record and the census changes with it. The records themselves are one file per
			product, each carrying its manufacturer source and the date it was checked; the
			full set is browsable in the <a href="/product-families/">product database</a>,
			and the rules a record must meet to earn a page are the same rules that produced
			the {indexable.length}/{rowsOnly} split above. The next census runs when the
			record count moves enough to change the story.
		</p>
	</article>
</PortalLayout>

<style>
	.feature {
		max-width: 76ch;
	}

	.kicker {
		font-size: var(--fs-xs);
		letter-spacing: 0.08em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		color: var(--accent);
		font-weight: 600;
		margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
	}

	.feature h1 {
		font-size: var(--fs-display);
		line-height: 1.2;
	}

	.feature h2 {
		margin-top: 1.6rem;
	}

	.quick-answer p,
	.limits p {
		margin: 0;
	}

	.table-wrap {
		margin-bottom: 1rem;
	}
</style>
